{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789564146,"nonce":"uGhFYKT+vydLGc4bKbJbVV6pG/o=","title":"Speculative LLVM callee promotion continues after the hot call ends","message":"When an OSR request promotes a function, tiered_backend's compile closure walks every eligible static callee and requests top-tier promotion. These callee requests are not tied to runtime demand. The broker may compile a transitive chain after the caller and even the benchmark have finished. At shutdown its promotion worker is joined, so queued work extends process wall time.\n\nWith LLVM enabled and ZYNTAX_OSR_TRACE=1 on nbody_modified, the existing call-heavy gate produces nine tier-2 recompiles across 12 trials. Removing static callee fan-out leaves three direct OSR requests. A diagnostic run that allowed advance promotion generated still more helper requests, with LLVM installs continuing after the timings printed. Ash's llvm_demand checks a hot loop, live recursive frame, or a caller loop before LLVM; it also gates on an IR-derived optimization ceiling. Zyntax has the ABI and call-heavy gates but no equivalent remaining-work signal.\n\nRemove static callee fan-out and invalidate beads on runtime shutdown so queued jobs can be skipped. Keep the broader HIR headroom/demand policy in dbe58cb228f043aa957061e5bea6f2e52823e1d5e3b4c6e0832b0e709318ad70: direct OSR requests still happen at entry and may compile short-lived functions after completion.\n\nChecked --fast comparisons: before, nbody_modified 320.45 ms mean and 2004 ms process wall, float 3.27 ms, richards 1.33 ms; after removing fan-out, nbody_modified 323.71 ms and 2045 ms wall, float 3.30 ms, richards 1.31 ms. A later run with shutdown invalidation gave nbody_modified 278.63 ms, float 3.52 ms, richards 1.30 ms. These short runs vary enough that no steady-state speedup is claimed. Compiler LLVM-enabled release suite passed.","files":null}]}